Id: no-source-content-off-this-server
Pattern: (\b[A-Za-z]*Export[A-Za-z]*[[:space:]]*\(|\b[A-Za-z]*Upload[A-Za-z]*[[:space:]]*\(|\b(Telemetry|Analytics|CrashReport|TrackEvent|ApplicationInsights|SentrySdk)[A-Za-z]*\b)
Subject: *.cs
Except: none
Issue: 82

A source's terms forbid passing its content on, and `docs/sources/tmdb.md` says
what may leave this server: content reaches the users of the server that fetched
it and reaches nothing else, identifiers may cross to another plugin on the same
server, and nothing aggregates content across installations. Under that rule the
page names four features as refused rather than leaving each one to be argued
when somebody proposes it: an export of the catalogue in any format, artwork
served from this server, a cache or index shared between installations, and
telemetry that carries a field a response supplied.

Until this rule landed, that list was read by a person or not at all, and the
page said so in the sentence this rule replaces. A refusal nothing refuses is
prose, and prose is what a proposal argues with.

What the pattern catches is the two of those four that a name gives away.

An export is the first. Whatever format somebody reaches for - a file, a
document, an archive, a route that hands the catalogue back - the member doing
it is called some kind of export, because that is what the person writing it
believes they are adding. The same goes for the shared cache and the direction
that feeds it: a member that uploads is a member sending what this server holds
somewhere this server is not. Neither word appears anywhere in this codebase
today, and neither has a legitimate use here: nothing this plugin does is an
export, because the surface is how content reaches a user and the seam in #94
passes identifiers rather than the record.

Telemetry is the second, by the names the shape arrives under. Analytics, crash
reporting and the two hosted collectors somebody reaches for first are the same
feature: a payload leaving the server on a schedule nobody browsing asked for.
The refusal is not against measuring anything - it is against a payload that
carries a title, a description or any other field a response supplied, and a
name-shaped pattern cannot read a payload, so it refuses the component instead.
An operator-facing diagnostic is a different thing and is #110, which stays on
the server and carries counts, times and identifiers.

What it cannot do, beyond the bound every rule here carries.

Two of the four refused features are outside it. Artwork served from this server
is not a name: it is bytes taking a route, and the shape that would produce it
is a fetch of an image the catalogue only holds a location for. What holds that
today is `no-network-outside-source-adapter`, which keeps every outbound call
inside one type, and #62, which is why the record carries a location rather than
an image. A shared cache is half held: the word that feeds one is in the pattern,
the reading side is a call to a host, and which hosts a source has is declared on
its terms page rather than in a rule here.

It reads names and never payloads. A member called something else that does
exactly what an export does passes this rule, and so does a legitimate export
that never arrives because none is wanted. The list on the terms page is still
what says which features are refused; this rule makes two of them cost a red run
rather than a conversation.
